1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,905
This thin strand carries light, yet an
enormous number of conversations

2
00:00:04,905 --> 00:00:09,810
can cross it together. How can the receiver
tell them apart?

3
00:00:09,810 --> 00:00:14,714
Imagine four senders all insisting on using
the same fiber at

4
00:00:14,714 --> 00:00:19,619
the same time. We could build four separate
paths, but the

5
00:00:19,619 --> 00:00:24,524
more interesting solution is to give each
signal a property that

6
00:00:24,524 --> 00:00:29,429
survives the journey. Today, that property is
wavelength. Watch the four

7
00:00:29,429 --> 00:00:34,333
colors; each represents a separate optical
channel.

8
00:00:34,333 --> 00:00:39,362
Each sender has its own changing stream of
bits. For now,

9
00:00:39,362 --> 00:00:44,390
we draw four separate paths so their
identities are obvious. The

10
00:00:44,390 --> 00:00:49,419
blue signal might carry one stream of data
while the green

11
00:00:49,419 --> 00:00:54,448
signal carries another. They needn't contain
the same pattern, begin together,

12
00:00:54,448 --> 00:00:59,476
or tell the same story. The problem is to
combine them

13
00:00:59,476 --> 00:01:04,505
physically while leaving the receiver enough
information to reconstruct each stream.

14
00:01:04,505 --> 00:01:09,533
Simply remembering that there were four
senders isn't enough.

15
00:01:09,533 --> 00:01:14,371
First look at a wave rather than its data.
Wavelength is

16
00:01:14,371 --> 00:01:19,210
the distance between repeating points, such as
neighboring crests. Compress the

17
00:01:19,210 --> 00:01:24,048
spacing and the wavelength gets shorter;
spread the crests and it

18
00:01:24,048 --> 00:01:28,886
gets longer. Our colors make that difference
easy to follow. Real

19
00:01:28,886 --> 00:01:33,724
communication systems commonly use infrared
light, so these display colors are

20
00:01:33,724 --> 00:01:38,562
labels for our eyes. They are not a promise
that the

21
00:01:38,562 --> 00:01:43,400
fiber contains these exact visible colors.

22
00:01:43,400 --> 00:01:48,114
A channel needs to carry something more
interesting than a perfectly

23
00:01:48,114 --> 00:01:52,829
unchanging wave. A transmitter varies
properties of its optical signal to

24
00:01:52,829 --> 00:01:57,543
represent information. Here, brightness
changes illustrate that idea: the pattern
changes

25
00:01:57,543 --> 00:02:02,257
while the channel keeps its wavelength
identity. Real modulation can be

26
00:02:02,257 --> 00:02:06,971
more sophisticated. Keep those two jobs
separate in your mind: modulation

27
00:02:06,971 --> 00:02:11,686
carries the information, while the wavelength
helps identify which optical channel

28
00:02:11,686 --> 00:02:16,400
we are looking at.

29
00:02:16,400 --> 00:02:21,305
Suppose we deliberately remove the difference
between the channels. Now the

30
00:02:21,305 --> 00:02:26,210
receiver cannot separate them merely by
choosing a wavelength, because they

31
00:02:26,210 --> 00:02:31,114
occupy the same wavelength region. Combining
signals only helps when there

32
00:02:31,114 --> 00:02:36,019
is a usable separation rule. The problem isn't
that two messages

33
00:02:36,019 --> 00:02:40,924
dislike each other. The receiver has lost the
clue that distinguished

34
00:02:40,924 --> 00:02:45,829
them. Give the channels different wavelength
allocations again, and that clue

35
00:02:45,829 --> 00:02:50,733
returns without adding another strand of
fiber.

36
00:02:50,733 --> 00:02:55,638
Place the four channels at separate positions
on this wavelength axis.

37
00:02:55,638 --> 00:03:00,543
Each position stands for a narrow optical
channel with room for

38
00:03:00,543 --> 00:03:05,448
its modulated signal. The spaces between them
matter: actual signals and

39
00:03:05,448 --> 00:03:10,352
optical filters don't have infinitely sharp
boundaries. If we pack channels

40
00:03:10,352 --> 00:03:15,257
too closely for the equipment, separating them
becomes harder. Wavelength division

41
00:03:15,257 --> 00:03:20,162
is organized sharing of optical spectrum, not
permission to place an

42
00:03:20,162 --> 00:03:25,067
unlimited number of channels wherever we like.

43
00:03:25,067 --> 00:03:29,843
Now watch the multiplexer. Separate optical
inputs enter; a combined optical

44
00:03:29,843 --> 00:03:34,619
signal leaves through one path. None of our
four conversations has

45
00:03:34,619 --> 00:03:39,395
been turned into the others. They coexist in
the outgoing light

46
00:03:39,395 --> 00:03:44,171
because they occupy different wavelength
channels. This is the central move

47
00:03:44,171 --> 00:03:48,948
in wavelength-division multiplexing, usually
shortened to WDM. The name sounds elaborate,

48
00:03:48,948 --> 00:03:53,724
but the drawing has already done most of the
explaining: different

49
00:03:53,724 --> 00:03:58,500
wavelengths, together in one fiber.

50
00:03:58,500 --> 00:04:03,338
Inside the shared link, all four channels
propagate together. Follow one

51
00:04:03,338 --> 00:04:08,176
pulse from each input and notice that they can
occupy the

52
00:04:08,176 --> 00:04:13,014
fiber at the same time. They are not taking
four long

53
00:04:13,014 --> 00:04:17,852
turns. The colored paths are a visual aid
showing channel identity,

54
00:04:17,852 --> 00:04:22,690
rather than separate colored tubes hidden
inside the strand. One physical

55
00:04:22,690 --> 00:04:27,529
optical path is carrying several
distinguishable optical channels, and each
channel

56
00:04:27,529 --> 00:04:32,367
can carry its own changing information.

57
00:04:32,367 --> 00:04:36,890
At the far end, the demultiplexer reverses the
arrangement. Optical components

58
00:04:36,890 --> 00:04:41,414
direct different wavelength regions toward
their corresponding receivers. Blue returns to

59
00:04:41,414 --> 00:04:45,938
blue's output; green returns to green's
output. The useful achievement is

60
00:04:45,938 --> 00:04:50,462
not just that light reached the destination.
The receiver recovered the

61
00:04:50,462 --> 00:04:54,986
right stream at the right output. Keep an eye
on the

62
00:04:54,986 --> 00:04:59,510
pulse pattern: separation preserves the
identity needed to interpret the original

63
00:04:59,510 --> 00:05:04,033
data.

64
00:05:04,033 --> 00:05:08,681
Cover three outputs and inspect the remaining
one. Its receiver is

65
00:05:08,681 --> 00:05:13,329
interested in the selected wavelength channel
while the others continue through

66
00:05:13,329 --> 00:05:17,976
their own outputs. This is why the wavelength
allocation matters at

67
00:05:17,976 --> 00:05:22,624
both ends. The transmitter alone can't invent
a private rule and

68
00:05:22,624 --> 00:05:27,271
hope the receiver guesses it. Multiplexing and
demultiplexing form a coordinated

69
00:05:27,271 --> 00:05:31,919
system: combine according to an agreed
arrangement, then use that arrangement

70
00:05:31,919 --> 00:05:36,567
to separate again.

71
00:05:36,567 --> 00:05:41,214
Let's attach numbers to the picture. Suppose
each of our four

72
00:05:41,214 --> 00:05:45,862
channels carries one hundred gigabits per
second. One channel contributes one

73
00:05:45,862 --> 00:05:50,510
hundred; two contribute two hundred; four
contribute four hundred gigabits per

74
00:05:50,510 --> 00:05:55,157
second of combined raw rate. The sum comes
from parallel channel

75
00:05:55,157 --> 00:05:59,805
capacity. We haven't made each individual
channel four times faster. We've

76
00:05:59,805 --> 00:06:04,452
arranged four channels so their rates
contribute to the total at

77
00:06:04,452 --> 00:06:09,100
the same time.

78
00:06:09,100 --> 00:06:13,938
Four hundred gigabits per second is not four
hundred gigabytes per

79
00:06:13,938 --> 00:06:18,776
second. Eight bits make a byte, so dividing by
eight gives

80
00:06:18,776 --> 00:06:23,614
fifty gigabytes per second as the
corresponding raw byte rate. Even

81
00:06:23,614 --> 00:06:28,452
that is not a promised file-download speed.
Coding, framing, and other

82
00:06:28,452 --> 00:06:33,290
overhead consume part of the transmitted
information, and the rest of

83
00:06:33,290 --> 00:06:38,129
the system may impose limits. The
multiplication describes our optical channel

84
00:06:38,129 --> 00:06:42,967
model, not every application's measured
throughput.

85
00:06:42,967 --> 00:06:48,062
It is tempting to keep adding channels and
declare victory over

86
00:06:48,062 --> 00:06:53,157
physics. But the available spectrum, channel
spacing, amplification, noise, and receiver

87
00:06:53,157 --> 00:06:58,252
equipment constrain the arrangement. In the
picture, adding another channel means

88
00:06:58,252 --> 00:07:03,348
finding a usable place for its signal and a
way to

89
00:07:03,348 --> 00:07:08,443
recover it. More wavelength channels can
increase total capacity, but the

90
00:07:08,443 --> 00:07:13,538
equipment must support the whole transmission.
An extra colored line on

91
00:07:13,538 --> 00:07:18,633
a diagram is much easier than a working
optical link.

92
00:07:18,633 --> 00:07:23,471
Our equal-rate example was convenient, not
compulsory. Let two channels carry

93
00:07:23,471 --> 00:07:28,310
one hundred gigabits per second each and two
carry fifty each.

94
00:07:28,310 --> 00:07:33,148
The combined raw rate is now three hundred
gigabits per second.

95
00:07:33,148 --> 00:07:37,986
Nothing about the word multiplexing requires
equal contributions. This is the

96
00:07:37,986 --> 00:07:42,824
same sum written more generally: add the
individual channel rates. The

97
00:07:42,824 --> 00:07:47,662
diagram changes one channel's contribution at
a time, so the arithmetic

98
00:07:47,662 --> 00:07:52,500
stays attached to the physical arrangement.

99
00:07:52,500 --> 00:07:57,405
The scale becomes striking in real optical
research. In June twenty

100
00:07:57,405 --> 00:08:02,310
twenty-six, NICT reported a wideband
transmission demonstration over installed
fiber using

101
00:08:02,310 --> 00:08:07,214
more than a thousand wavelength channels. That
achievement also depended on

102
00:08:07,214 --> 00:08:12,119
amplification, modulation, and receiver
techniques; WDM did not produce the entire

103
00:08:12,119 --> 00:08:17,024
result by itself. Our four-channel drawing
isolates one essential idea inside

104
00:08:17,024 --> 00:08:21,929
that much richer system. The same separation
principle can operate at

105
00:08:21,929 --> 00:08:26,833
a scale far beyond four colored inputs.

106
00:08:26,833 --> 00:08:31,800
We began with four senders competing for one
fiber. The solution

107
00:08:31,800 --> 00:08:36,767
was to preserve a distinction the receiver
could use: wavelength. Give

108
00:08:36,767 --> 00:08:41,733
each channel a suitable optical allocation,
combine them, carry them together,

109
00:08:41,733 --> 00:08:46,700
and separate them again. The capacity
arithmetic follows the picture because

110
00:08:46,700 --> 00:08:51,667
several channels contribute simultaneously.
One strand can support many conversations when

111
00:08:51,667 --> 00:08:56,633
sharing does not destroy their identities.
That is the useful idea

112
00:08:56,633 --> 00:09:01,600
to keep when the long name fades away.
